In recent years, HUD's have been incorporated into more cars. Popular in airplanes, both real and simulated, HUD's provide information to the driver (or pilot) without forcing them to shift their line of sight. HUD's are not a new concept in cars, having been seen as early as 19885. In the past 20 years, advances in technology have allowed more information to be displayed, and in considerably better quality. Today, several different car companies - including BMW, Lexus, and GM to name a few have built HUD's into their vehicles. For cars without built-in HUD's, one can purchase 3rd party systems that can be installed to perform similar tasks.6 Over time, HUD technology has seen many improvements. For example, HUD's are now capable of adjusting to ambient light, so the images are just as clear whether you are driving at day or night.
